00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.360
and what are they
costing me, how

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.820
do I organize cost data so I can
do more useful things with it,

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.270
and how can I draw
insight with my cost data

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.890
that I can share with and
inform the right people,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.590
then let's take this
journey together.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.840
This is where you want to be.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.110
Now, we're going to explore
the questions I just

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.240
raised in four ways.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.270
First, an introduction,
or more specifically,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.850
how a company like Etsy
arrives at managing cost

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.550
data in the cloud and
why it's necessary.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.310
We'll then talk about some
simple and primary sources

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.180
of cost data and understand the
details of how it gets to you.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.400
Then we'll dive
into a few use cases

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.280
and their corresponding
interfaces or visual output

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.860
to help jump-start you in how
you might analyze your cost

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.580
data and make it useful to you.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.310
And then we'll finish
with a few tips

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.030
that you'll want to keep in mind
as you're managing your cost

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.630
data into the future.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.365
So let's start at the beginning.

00:02:06.365 --> 00:02:07.740
Many companies
have their reasons

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.157
for moving into
the cloud and then

00:02:09.157 --> 00:02:12.060
unsurprisingly, alongside the
lack of equipment management

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.683
and heavy IT footprints,
cost savings is one of them.

00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:17.100
Dany is going to
go ahead and talk

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.920
a little bit about the start
of Etsy's cloud journey.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.108
Dany?

00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:22.650
DANY DAYA: Thank
you very much, Debo.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.680
Let me start by sharing
an overview about how Etsy

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.750
decided to move to the cloud.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.590
For those who are not
familiar with Etsy,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.060
Etsy is like global marketplace
for unique and creative good.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.630
It's a home of a universe of
special extraordinary item

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.990
from unique, handcrafted
pieces to vintage items.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.040
There are more than 2.8
million active sellers on Etsy,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.740
and the number that I want
to draw your attention to

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.590
is that we have more than 66
million unique active listings

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:52.320
at Etsy.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.450
This massive number
of active listings

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.480
require advanced
machine-learning capabilities

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.460
and search
capabilities to ensure

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.070
that buyers can find
easily what they are

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.680
looking for on our platform.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.230
Such advanced capabilities
require more and more

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.430
infrastructure.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.250
And maintaining such
infrastructure in data centers

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.340
would limit Etsy's goal
in continuous growth.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.090
As a result of that, Etsy
started exploring cloud vendors

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.940
and moving to the cloud.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.600
In 2017, Etsy made
that strategic decision

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.280
to move to the cloud.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.390
We followed a very
complicated decision matrix

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.420
that contained more than
200 different factors

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.850
to evaluate different
cloud vendors.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.180
As a result of that, we
decided to move with GCP.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.420
If I want to summarize
all of those 800 factors

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.440
in kind of three main takeaways,
I would choose number one,

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.670
scalability.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.670
Scalability is the ability
to scale on demand.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.520
With data centers, you
have to plan ahead of time,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.950
probably six to
nine months ahead

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.590
to do activities such as
provisioning, purchase

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.680
of hardware, installing
hardware, testing them,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.850
and then running them.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.430
On the cloud, things
are much, much faster.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.520
You can do that in significantly
shorter time frame,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.330
and guess what?

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.970
You can scale down after you
finish using your resources.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.610
The second major thing
is sustainability.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.390
Sustainability is one of
the major goals at Etsy.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:23.550
We do track sustainability as
we track revenue and profit.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:27.390
We find in Google a partner
that is aligned on this topic.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:32.700
GCP commits to carbon neutrality
as well as energy efficiency.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.760
Also, Google leverages
the latest technology

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.290
to achieve those goals.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.470
This commitment made
us very comfortable

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.210
moving forward with Google.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.900
This sort of thing is around
creativity and enablement.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.320
We want to enable our
developers and engineers

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.870
to be able to use the latest
technologies out there

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.330
in the market in order
for them to continue

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:58.830
improving our platform and
serve our community of buyers

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.560
and sellers.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.410
So the decision
was made in 2017.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.200
In 2018, we started
the migration.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.810
And earlier in 2020,
we successfully

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.760
completed this migration.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.620
However, I would like
to share with you

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.980
and highlight some of the
non-engineering changes

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.540
that we went through.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.120
The first thing is the change
in the type of the cloud cost

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:23.070
itself.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.170
The cost on the
cloud is variable,

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.200
and it's very easy for
developers and engineers

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.210
to spend additional
resources, which

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.190
will lead to additional costs.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.260
On the contrary, in
the data center world,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.120
the costs were more
or less predictable

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.640
and managed by specific folks
in engineering and finance,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.310
and at the end of the day, the
availability of the resources

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.630
were limited by the
capacity of the data center.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.940
The second change is
associated with how

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.040
we purchase cloud resources.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.290
If you want to think about
that in a cloud word,

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.320
every developer or
engineer has the ability

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.210
to spend additional resources.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.990
In a way, we are having
a centralized procurement

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.840
of cloud resources, where
in the past everything

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.120
was centralized
between few folks

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.460
within engineering and finance.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.450
The third change is kind of
a result of the prior two.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.500
It's basically the
need of visibility.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.740
With the increased number of
users and all of those users

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.250
have different use cases
and needs for visibility,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.890
we found ourselves
that we have to provide

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.050
more visibility into
where the cost is

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.970
coming from on the cloud.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.760
All of that required
us to follow

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.010
sort of a decentralized
cloud budget, governance,

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.610
and forecasting methods.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.970
One of the way to
handle this challenges

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:46.340
is to adopt some of the cloud
cost governance or management

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.220
approaches.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.200
And I'll hand over
back to Debo now

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.195
to walk you through some
of those approaches.

00:06:54.195 --> 00:06:56.570
DEBO ADERIBIGBE: Thank you,
Dany, for that great lead-in.

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.880
It brings us back full
circle to the four

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.880
pillars that really define
Google Cloud Cost Management.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.820
The first, of course,
is visibility.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:08.050
That means that we provide you
a strong sense of transparency

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.400
through our interfaces,
documentation,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.040
and APIs to help
you be able to find

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.860
and understand your
costs and your usage.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.660
With accountability,
it means that we

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.490
enable you to
organize and allocate

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:22.190
your costs in ways that
align accountability

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.410
and budgets for the
appropriate business units

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.510
that you have in
your company or cost

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.840
centers or teams, whatever
makes the most sense.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.370
Control, of course,
means that you

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.950
can make costs more
predictable with tools

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.130
that help you forecast, budget,
and essentially mitigate risk.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.280
And finally, intelligence.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.870
That means we give
you recommendations

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:45.590
and optimizations to help you,
at core, save money because who

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.032
doesn't want to do that?

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:48.740
And so now that we've
talked a little bit

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.050
about how Google Cloud
Cost Management works

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.640
and how Etsy has really
gotten to this point,

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.540
let's talk a little bit
more about data sources.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.660
Hopefully you agree that
cost management in the cloud

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:00.930
is very important.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.540
Let's take a look at where
that data comes from.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.535
If we simplify, there are
three main simple methods

00:08:09.535 --> 00:08:12.120
to take a look at your
costs and billing data.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.470
In just a moment, we'll
dig deeper into each,

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.340
but we of course, have the
GCP invoice and statements,

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.820
we have the UI and the billing
console that contains reports,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.610
tables, and views,
and then finally, we

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.540
have the GCP BigQuery billing
export, the most granular

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:34.020
and frequently updated source
of data in this group of three.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.419
Accessible from the
Google Cloud console,

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:40.559
we have the GCP statements and
invoices on the documents page.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:44.710
With few exceptions, if you
set up as an invoice account,

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.550
you'll see these once a
month on the fifth day

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.710
in the month following
the month that you

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.200
were using your GCP services.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.510
Your invoice contains
information about you,

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:57.180
such as business
addresses, tax IDs,

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.920
as well as taxes or payments
for usage made within the month.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.420
On it, the duration
of the invoice

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.820
month, you'll see
the highest level

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.310
of aggregation of
costs and products

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.300
and SKUs in terms of
your cost information.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.050
If you're looking for
basic cost data that

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.520
can be handled and handed
over to finance, accounting,

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.800
and legal for simple bottom-line
numbers, this may be enough.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.220
But as they say, if you
give a mouse a cookie,

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.500
they're probably going
to want a glass of milk.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.840
So the billing console
in Cost Management

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.220
is where you're going to go.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.130
The billing console has a
number of interface experiences

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:36.710
that help you to slice and
dice your data appropriately.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.060
This includes interfaces
such as the cost overview

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:43.600
to see cost trends at a
glance, the cost table, which

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.930
serves as a more manipulable
form of your invoice,

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.020
and cost reports, which
allow you to filter,

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.470
aggregate, and zero in
on specific attributes

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.120
to do analysis.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.570
The key insight I want
you to take away here

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.070
is that the things you are able
to do in the billing console

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.900
are the greatest
common denominator

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.290
amongst many types of customers
and companies that we serve.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:07.597
We realize that one
size doesn't fit all,

00:10:07.597 --> 00:10:09.430
and so we're working
constantly to make sure

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.110
that we improve our
views to make sure

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.360
that they fit most people.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.010
Now, when you really
need to go custom

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:17.560
or you're missing a
particular view or insight

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.640
that you want to
take advantage of,

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:22.800
that's when you want to go to
the billing BigQuery export.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.890
And hint, you're going to hear
me say this more than once,

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.190
you've got to turn it on.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.107
It is an enabled feature.

00:10:29.107 --> 00:10:30.940
So turn it on to make
sure that you have it.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.800
You're going to thank me later.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:34.510
The billing BigQuery
export enables

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:36.520
you to export detailed
Google Cloud billing

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.450
data, such as usage, pricing,
and cost estimate data

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.340
automatically through
the day to a BigQuery

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.680
data set that you specify.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.680
You can then do all
kinds of things with it.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.970
You can query it directly
for a specific insight,

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:52.090
or you could use additional
tools to build and visualize

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:54.130
data and look at dashboards.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.080
You might also export
data to other formats

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:01.030
such as JSON or CSV to do other
programmatic manipulations.

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.160
Think of it as the
most customizable form

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.510
of your billing data.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.640
Now, in the building and
monetization team at GCP,

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.450
we take the data in
and it flows like this.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.220
We get usage at
different intervals,

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.220
depending on which
GCP service you're

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:20.880
talking about, such as GCS,
our storage service, Kubernetes

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.760
Engine, or Compute Engine,
and in a simplified sense,

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.720
we take that data in and
tally up the total usage we've

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.600
received and determine how
much it should cost based

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.350
on a number of factors,
such as price, discounting,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:36.480
and other modifications.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.940
That information then
takes multiple paths.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:42.510
For one, we have a path to
payments to prepare invoices,

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:46.470
and then we also have two
paths to our reporting engine

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:50.970
for usage of the UI, and
then also the indexing engine

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:55.260
to make sure that we display
an export respectively.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.850
Now, note that for what
matters to you, the export

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:02.970
updates more frequently.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.580
Taking one more minute to
talk about that export,

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:10.170
one of its most special
powers is its flexibility.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.630
Oh my, another hint?

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.860
Maybe you should turn it on.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.100
Beyond the ability to write SQL
queries to integrate very, very

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.160
well with your other
visualization products,

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.840
you can use tools like Data
Studio or Looker or Tableau

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:26.597
to visualize this
data in a way that

00:12:26.597 --> 00:12:29.180
makes it more effective to share
with others instead of having

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.090
them go directly into the
BigQuery tables and partitions

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:36.060
to understand the insights
that you'd like to share.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:39.270
Now, it's also a source for
third-party integrators.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:43.275
So if you're looking to use an
integrator like CloudHealth,

00:12:43.275 --> 00:12:45.150
you might be able to
give them these insights

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:46.620
through your
BigQuery so they can

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.650
provide more multicloud
insights for you.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:54.430
In the end, the BigQuery billing
export is very versatile.

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.750
So we've talked about
where your data comes from,

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.170
and I suppose it's time that we
think about crawling, walking,

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.500
and running with specific
use cases that more match up

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.190
to your everyday views of
how you deal with cost data.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:11.130
How do we make sense
of all of this?

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.390
For a single use
case, let's go back

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.850
to one of the key questions
that we started with.

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:21.280
How can I organize my cost data
and make it easier to manage?

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:25.200
Well, to answer that question,
we got to start with a crawl.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.500
And that crawl portion
of this use case

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.450
means understanding that
we like to call here

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:33.335
at Google Cloud the
flexible billing hierarchy.

00:13:33.335 --> 00:13:34.960
The billing hierarchy
starts at the top

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.280
with the billing account.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.220
It's one of your largest and
most important containers

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:40.750
for cost data.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.750
We say that because it's the
level responsible for paying

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.482
for all of the resources
you use at GCP services.

00:13:46.482 --> 00:13:48.190
So it's a fairly large
container that you

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.350
can use to assign to costs.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:52.300
Underneath that
is a folder level.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:55.360
It's commonly mapped to
larger business units and cost

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.190
centers, such as
DevOps or engineering.

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:01.872
And then you move on
to the project level.

00:14:01.872 --> 00:14:03.830
This is the most common
container for resources

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:05.990
directly because
it's a good scoping

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:09.530
mechanism for individual teams
or for resources that you want

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:13.250
to keep together so you can do
other things like set access

00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:15.530
policies or a label.

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:18.530
Below these are resources,
and examples of resources

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:21.920
include things like databases,
Kubernetes clusters,

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:23.820
and virtual machines.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:27.750
Remember that a resource can
have one and only one project.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.980
So if you want a way to identify
resources that span projects,

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.950
you want to make
effective use of labels.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:36.200
Really, metadata that
you could use for,

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:39.980
among many other reasons,
costing your resources

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.780
and identifying them together.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.650
Once you've understood all
this, you can start to walk.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:50.130
All walking involves here is
diving into some of the billing

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:53.940
console changes that
you might find in terms

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:55.800
of attributes of your data.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.570
And if you'd like to see it in a
specific way or to your liking,

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:02.700
you can use some of the filters
that appear in our interfaces

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.030
in Cost Management.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:07.230
So whether it's something
like timing, usage or export

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.970
time, location, the region
your resources are running in

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.160
or other types of locations,
or container level, project

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.940
or folder, you can filter
to make the view exposing

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:19.380
information that you need.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.845
And you can do quite the
same with the export,

00:15:22.845 --> 00:15:24.970
except that it's a little
bit different of a format

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:26.200
and interface.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.250
Here, if we take a brief look
at the BigQuery interface,

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:30.870
you can see that you
can write queries

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:33.150
to find very
specific information

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.490
that's custom to your needs.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:39.180
And in the resulting data sets,
you can see all or just a few

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:41.070
of the attributes
in the table schema

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.500
that you want to see,
from the billing account

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:47.940
to usage to cost data,
right on down to labels.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.550
But these are just
some of the basics.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:54.000
Once you have all the
data, you can really run.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.710
Let's switch over
to Dany so you can

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.260
get an idea of what advance
cost management looks like,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.840
specifically for Etsy.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:02.430
DANY DAYA: Thank
you very much, Debo.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:05.520
Actually, before start running,
I want to highlight one thing.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.970
The console Google make, of GCP
console that Debo mentioned,

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:09.990
is really powerful.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:13.080
And I personally love
using it, especially when

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:16.140
it comes to forecasting
your future spend.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:19.200
It has a lot of features
that I encourage all of you

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:20.350
to go try it.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:26.700
However, at Etsy we have big
number of people using GCP,

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.220
and all of those people
are interested in knowing

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:31.770
their costs and
understanding it better.

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:36.040
To build such tools to enable
us to monitor at scale,

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:39.090
we had to rely on
two major inputs.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.370
The first one, as you heard Debo
mentioning, the billing export,

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:46.350
which include a lot of the
data on the billing that's

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:47.490
happening on Google site.

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.650
So, as Debo mentioned,
please turn it on

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.640
if you plan on using
it in the future.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:53.850
The second thing is
something we are calling it

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:57.180
as business inputs that
are specific to Etsy.

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:01.620
Think about stuff like a budget,
like a team, how are folders

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:02.340
and-- or sorry.

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:05.160
How GCP projects
are grouped together

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:06.970
under one team or cost center.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:09.480
And I'll walk you through
an example of that in a bit.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.420
We take those two inputs,
we merge them in BigQuery,

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.109
then we export the
output to Looker.

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:17.849
And Looker is a tool that
will enable us to slice

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:20.140
and dice the data as we like.

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:22.720
And I'm going to walk you
through those details in a bit.

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:24.990
So when I talk about
business inputs,

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:26.520
what am I talking about, really?

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:28.900
I'm sharing here with
you couple of example.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:32.340
The first one is
mapping GCP projects

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.800
to business units or
teams or cost centers.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.930
So what you're
seeing here is one,

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:40.560
we're representing all of
our-- or actually one column

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.510
representing all of
our GCP projects,

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.280
and the second column
is representing

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:48.270
all of the teams
or business units

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:53.160
that are running or owning
that specific GCP project.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:56.730
Another example is
assigning a specific budget

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.890
for each business unit.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.680
Again, we assign budgets
on monthly basis.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:03.930
Your organization
might be doing it

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.940
on quarterly basis
or annual basis.

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.970
The goal here is
just to show you

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.310
how we can merge
all of this data

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.590
together with the billing data.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:17.220
One thing that always
popped to my mind

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:20.880
is, what is the difference
between the labeling

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.650
and what Debo
presented and what we

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.890
are talking about here at Etsy?

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:28.180
In my opinion, those are
kind of the same thing.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:31.470
It's just like at Etsy,
we decided to not burden

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:36.200
our engineers with the task
of maintaining and labeling

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:39.420
and continuing to label
resources as things change.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:43.440
We decided to keep that
centralized under the program

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:44.430
management team.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.860
What kind of output
we are looking

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.350
at when it comes to Looker?

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:52.230
The first thing is we
send automated emails

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:56.820
to the different stakeholders
who have interest in GCP costs.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:01.200
Such emails notification could
include a variety of data.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:03.810
What we are looking
here is a sample of one

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:06.750
of those graphs included
in the daily emails.

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:10.760
In fact, this graph, it
shows the account daily

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:15.920
spend broken down by GCP
service versus the budget.

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:20.120
So each column in this graph
represents the daily GCP spend.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:24.980
Furthermore, we break each
daily spend by the GCP service.

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:27.560
And in this case, we are
looking at different colors.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:31.250
Each color representing
a different GCP service.

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:33.860
The green is for Google Compute.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:37.420
The orange is for cloud storage.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:42.530
And if you can see, there is
light vertical black line.

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:44.770
This line represents
the daily budget.

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:48.040
Whenever a column
exceeds that line,

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.140
it means we have
a budget overrun.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.110
Whenever the column is
below that budget line,

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:56.850
it means we have a budget
surplus on that specific date.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.465
Now, this is another
interesting thing

00:19:58.465 --> 00:19:59.590
I want to highlight to you.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:02.720
Once you have all of
this data in Looker,

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.770
it becomes very easy
to visualize this data

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:07.520
in different ways.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:09.460
And for a person
like me who is not

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:13.660
a big fan of
querying and coding,

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.710
Looker comes very, very
handy and useful to me.

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:22.340
With Looker, I can apply
specific filters and the change

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:25.250
specific selections
to transfer the graph

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:28.460
that we looked at earlier
into this graph, which

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:31.610
is the daily costs,
daily GCP costs.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.890
Rather than looking at
the account program,

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:36.470
we are looking at the
specific business unit.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:40.130
And rather than looking at
breakdown by GCP service,

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:43.470
we are looking at
breakdown by GCP project.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:47.690
So an engineering manager
could look at this graph

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:51.710
and be able to track the
spend of every project

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:54.530
within his or her organization.

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:56.060
With that, I'm
going to hand back

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.730
to Debo to talk more about
some of the more advanced

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.870
Google monitoring tools.

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:04.120
DEBO ADERIBIGBE: Thanks, Dany.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.340
Those graphs are pretty cool,
and they're informative.

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:09.460
So let's kind of stay
on the subject of budget

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:10.585
since you brought us there.

00:21:10.585 --> 00:21:12.543
And you're probably asking
yourselves out there

00:21:12.543 --> 00:21:14.020
in the audience,
is there anything

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:18.250
I can do in GCP to not just
budget but give insights

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:20.320
on how our budgets are
doing and performing

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:22.810
and inform the people who
need to know about them?

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:25.540
It's a great question, and
we do have a little bit

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:28.380
to help you here in GCP.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.300
In fact, GCP Cost
Management gives you a place

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.100
to do it in a fundamental way.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.255
You can set budgets through
the budgets interface

00:21:35.255 --> 00:21:36.880
in the billing console,
and when you're

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:39.610
ready to walk with this
data, you can set things

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.287
like automated alerts.

00:21:41.287 --> 00:21:43.120
You can set things like
cloud functions that

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:47.680
will manipulate your resources
when thresholds are reached,

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:51.340
and you can get and set
these budgets with APIs

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.140
and compare that
budget information

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:55.180
to what you have in
your usage and cost data

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.300
that you've gotten from your
billing BigQuery export.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:01.372
But Dany and Etsy are once
again way ahead of the curve.

00:22:01.372 --> 00:22:02.830
They've played with
these features,

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.150
and I think they love them.

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.818
Dany can confirm
that one for you.

00:22:05.818 --> 00:22:07.360
But they've gone a
little bit further

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:10.660
to develop strategies that
work well for their business.

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.700
Dany, why don't you show
us what Etsy does when

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:15.330
you're running with that data?

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:15.970
DANY DAYA: Yep.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:17.440
Let's do that.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.710
So I'm going to walk you through
three different approaches

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:23.065
or three different
layers of tracking

00:22:23.065 --> 00:22:26.800
the variation or the
trends in our GCP spend.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:29.930
The first one, I look at
it as an account level.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:32.590
So what you are looking
at here on this graph

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:36.850
is the daily, as well
as month-to-date,

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:39.470
variation from budget.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:42.010
So if you look at
the orange bars, what

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:46.150
those orange bars are presents
is the daily variation

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:47.230
from budget.

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:51.400
If the bar is above zero, it
means we have a budget overrun.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:53.360
And some of you
might be like, OK,

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:55.215
what does that even mean to me?

00:22:55.215 --> 00:22:57.350
I'll try to explain it to you.

00:22:57.350 --> 00:22:59.130
So if you look at
this graph, you

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:01.180
can see in the first
few days of the month,

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:04.390
we were having
positive orange bars.

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:06.880
However, at one point,
we realized at Etsy

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:09.082
that this is something
that's not a good trend.

00:23:09.082 --> 00:23:10.040
And we don't want that.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:13.750
So we went and investigated
what's the cause of that budget

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:14.490
overrun.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.660
And we realized that
this is not the best

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.250
way of investing our money.

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:22.210
So we ended up killing some
of the workflows that are

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:23.860
causing this budget overrun.

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:26.680
And this is reflected in this
graph in kind of the middle

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:30.170
where you see that the
orange bars are now negative,

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.350
which means that
we have a budget

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:34.060
surplus on a daily level.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:38.530
And also, it's represented
in the green line

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:41.620
that reflects the
monthly variation

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.510
as a percent from the budget.

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:46.840
It started high because we were
significantly above budget,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.720
and it starts to go way
down towards the end

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:52.210
of the month, which is
what we intended on doing.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:55.630
You can imagine that people
at finance and accounting

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:57.400
would appreciate
this kind of graph

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:59.680
because they love
tracking budgets.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:03.070
But this graph might
not be super actionable

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:07.330
for engineering managers who are
interested more at the project

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:08.150
level.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.850
So for this kind
of audience, I like

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:13.660
to introduce a
new dashboard that

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:18.850
shows that daily variation of
costs at the project level.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:21.090
So if you look at
the upper graph here,

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:25.470
you are seeing all the project,
or actually, the top 15 or 10

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:28.290
projects that have the
biggest change day-to-day.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:31.410
And the bars here present
whether the budget

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:34.200
or the cost of this
project increase day-to-day

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:35.460
or decrease.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:39.300
This information can come very
useful for engineering managers

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:43.050
and guide them to the
point or the project that's

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:45.690
causing the budget variation.

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:48.870
They might not need to take
any corrective actions,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:50.780
but the data is there for them.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:52.290
The second graph,
and again, this

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:56.220
is one of the beautiful
features with Looker,

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:58.330
is giving similar
information here.

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:00.520
But rather than looking
at the project level,

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:02.710
we are looking at the
business unit level.

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:05.490
And rather than looking
at the daily change,

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.590
we are looking at
the monthly change.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:10.620
So this graph provide
input for a director

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:12.465
to be able-- or
like a VP, probably,

00:25:12.465 --> 00:25:17.040
to be able to understand which
business unit is changing

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:18.870
its spend months over months.

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:21.510
All of those gives us
an idea about finance

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:24.850
or like whole-level,
account-level, project-level,

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:28.110
but in reality, the
most important part

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.290
in this hierarchy
are the individuals,

00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:33.750
the individuals who are
actually running the workflows.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.570
And although the title
on the slide says run,

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:39.070
I need to tell you
that this is a sprint.

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:42.210
This is a lot of data
that we combine together.

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:45.180
The first two we talked about
earlier, the billing export

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.200
as well as the business
input from Etsy.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:51.670
The third one that we introduced
here is resource labeling.

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:53.880
So why are we introducing
resource labeling?

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:55.920
The reason why we
introduce resource labeling

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:59.970
is that we have different
developers using the same GCP

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:00.860
project.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:05.160
And within each project, each
developer or product initiative

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:07.260
is using specific resources.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:10.890
So we need to associate
that specific resource

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:14.940
with the individual or the
product initiative that

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.460
is using it or
spending that resource.

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:20.220
And for that, we use a
specific automated tooling

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:21.840
to provide this labeling.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:23.820
Once we have all of
this data, again,

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:25.680
we merge it together in Looker.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.500
We are able to visualize
this heat map, which

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:33.870
shows the trailing five-day
cost for all of those workflows

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:36.600
that are either associated
with that individual

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:39.510
or with a specific
product initiative.

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:42.080
Also, we are using color-coding.

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:44.670
The color-coding makes
it much easier for people

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:47.340
to spot any changes
and anomalies.

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:50.370
In this case, if
for example, one row

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:52.680
that's representing
specific developer,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.990
their color has
always been green.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.070
If one day their
color is now red,

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:59.310
it means they are
spending more, and they

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:02.640
need to at least analyze
what's happening.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:04.770
They might not need to
take any corrective actions

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.870
because some of that
spend is expected,

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:08.950
and we want it to happen.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:11.430
So now that I gave you
an overview about how

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:17.150
we track and to trend the
budget variation at Etsy,

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:20.800
I want to be very
clear and realistic

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:22.730
in setting expectations.

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:27.260
It took us a long time to set
and put all of those monitoring

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:28.830
tools in place.

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:32.990
Understanding and mastering
the cloud in general, the cloud

00:27:32.990 --> 00:27:35.280
billing, is not an easy task.

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:37.460
So it took us time,
and I would like

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:40.040
to share with you
some of the lessons

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:43.690
that Etsy learned
through this journey.

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:47.890
The first one is, try to focus
on one service at a time.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:52.210
Depending on your account size
and your level of understanding

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.700
of cloud cost and
cloud management,

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:58.840
it might be really overwhelming
to try to understand everything

00:27:58.840 --> 00:27:59.920
at the same time.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:04.540
So my tip for you is strive to
focus on the main cost drivers

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:05.380
first.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:09.650
And that could be either just be
service or a specific project.

00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:12.100
Once you have a better
understanding of those,

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.980
try to scale them to
the tail of the spend.

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:17.590
The second thing that I
would encourage you to do

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:22.180
is test any monitoring or any
reporting with stakeholders.

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:25.480
At the end of the day, you might
have different stakeholders.

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:28.990
Each of them might want
different things to focus on.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:31.810
So it's really important
to get those people aligned

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:35.260
and to hear their feedback when
you are doing your reporting

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:36.010
and monitoring.

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.980
At the end of the day, they are
the end-users of this product,

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:42.350
and you want to make sure that
they are on-board with it.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:45.710
The third thing is
iterate and innovate.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:50.500
Cloud cost management,
it's not a one-time action.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:52.480
As long as you are
using the cloud,

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:57.710
you will have to revisit your
monitoring and reporting needs.

00:28:57.710 --> 00:29:00.590
There might be a lot of reasons
why you might have to do that.

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:04.620
I'm just going to mention one
example, which happened lately

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:05.120
with us.

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:09.210
Google introduced a new product,
which is BigQuery Reservation.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:13.410
And again, it's a product that
many users needs to use it.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:16.130
And we realize that we
need to reflect with them,

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:18.710
and show the users how
much they are spending.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:20.940
So we had to go
back to our worker

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.660
and try to slice and
dice this data again

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:25.690
to make it available for them.

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:28.680
With time, we realized we
become more familiar with it,

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:31.040
and you are very easily--

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.860
easier for you to
build those dashboard.

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:35.450
DEBO ADERIBIGBE:
Well, thanks, Dany,

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:36.860
for all the work
that you've done

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:40.290
in sharing what your
running style is with Etsy

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.340
and working with your cost data
to make it especially useful

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:44.450
for you.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:46.213
I have a little bit
of parting advice

00:29:46.213 --> 00:29:47.880
to supplement that
information that Dany

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:50.090
has given you to make
sure that you get the most

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:52.040
out of your cost
data as you continue

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:53.435
on that lifelong journey.

00:29:53.435 --> 00:29:55.550
Or maybe not lifelong,
but at least as long

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:57.063
as you're working
with that business

00:29:57.063 --> 00:29:58.480
to make sure that
you get the cost

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:01.290
data to be correct and
accurate and useful.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:03.260
So the first, again,
I promised you

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:05.480
I was going to say it
several times, turn

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:07.410
billing BigQuery export on.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:09.760
It's an extremely
useful data set,

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:12.380
and in terms of making sure that
you have it available for you

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:15.140
from the beginning of your cloud
journey to wherever you may be

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:17.090
now in the present
and into the future,

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:19.940
it has to be enabled so that
you can generate the data set

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.670
and have it appear
where you need it to be.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:26.270
Secondly, you should analyze
your cost detail and reports

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:28.850
instead of using the
invoice for analysis.

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:30.740
I've heard it commonly
amongst many customers

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:33.365
that they looked at the invoice
to try to get a better sense of

00:30:33.365 --> 00:30:35.210
whether or not their
costs were accurate,

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:38.270
but really, we even got a cost
detail table that actually

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.210
mimics very closely the
amount of information that's

00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:44.780
in the invoice and then allows
you to selectively filter

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:47.480
and look at more granular
versions of that data

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:49.700
to the point where it's
going to help you and be

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:52.750
more accurate and active
at the moment in given time

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:54.150
that you're looking at it.

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:56.000
Then, of course,
want to make sure you

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:57.350
have a labeling strategy.

00:30:57.350 --> 00:30:59.360
You'll really thank
me for this one later.

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:03.020
Labeling allows you to cross-cut
and deliver certain attributes

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.790
upon which we'd
like to make sure

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:07.970
that there's accountability and
cost data specifically tagged

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:08.570
to.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:10.760
So make sure that
your labeling strategy

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:12.740
has a little bit
of flexibility so

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:14.570
that if your business
changed, let's say

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:16.400
you go through a
reorg or you need

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:18.730
to do something new in
terms of the applications

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:21.080
you develop, that you're
labeling strategy is not

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:24.050
so pigeonholing that you
can't change it and still

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:26.780
have it tell you the type
of cost data that you need.

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:29.240
Then, watch out for
querying gotchas.

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:31.340
I hear often from
customers that they

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:34.230
looked at usage time instead
of the time of export,

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:37.760
which is almost a slice of
time where you're querying

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:39.920
to see when the data
was exported as opposed

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:42.968
to what was driving its costs
at the time that it was used.

00:31:42.968 --> 00:31:45.260
So make sure you use the
right one when you're actually

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:46.052
writing your query.

00:31:46.052 --> 00:31:47.460
So look closely.

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:50.545
Then finally, create
a query library.

00:31:50.545 --> 00:31:52.670
You'll find that sometimes,
when you're using tools

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.920
like Looker or Tableau,
that a query is

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:57.500
the basis of a window
into the information

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:59.810
or view that you want to
create that will eventually

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:02.250
become a dashboard as
that information matures.

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:04.250
So if you can keep a
collection of those things,

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:07.430
they'll always
remain useful for you

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.410
in terms of making
decisions later

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:11.270
about what you'd like
to do and who you'd

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:13.190
like to share that data with.

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:15.715
So with that, we
come to our close.

00:32:15.715 --> 00:32:17.840
There's plenty you can
learn about Cost Management,

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.970
and we hoped that you learned a
lot from hearing from us today,

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:22.880
but just in case you
couldn't get enough,

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:24.890
we do have another
session about what's

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:28.580
new in Cost Management
and the cloud, CST 101,

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:30.080
that I hope you take a look at.

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:31.695
And below on the
slide, I've given you

00:32:31.695 --> 00:32:33.070
several resources
that might help

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:35.240
you more familiarize
with cost management

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:37.700
in general and Looker
and other tools

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:40.620
that might be useful for you in
your cost management journey.

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:43.282
But in the meantime, I want
to make sure that I thank you,

00:32:43.282 --> 00:32:44.990
the audience, for
giving us your eyeballs

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:47.900
and your ears and your time, and
say, this is Debo signing off.

00:32:47.900 --> 00:32:50.305
And Dany, hope you get
to say goodbye too.

